# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*
# ...except the template, which holds no secrets and is the setup instructions
# for the rental contract mailer.
!.env.local.example
!.env.preview.template

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# prisma client, generated from prisma/schema.prisma
# Prisma 7 generates into the project rather than into node_modules, so it has
# to be ignored explicitly. `pnpm install` and `pnpm build` both regenerate it.
/generated/

out.zip

# duplicate extracted copy of the site (identical to working tree) and its archive
/New folder/
website.rar

# windows/cygwin crash dumps
*.stackdump
# The transcribed pre-backend contracts.
#
# Real renters' names, birth dates, addresses, phone numbers and email
# addresses for seven people. Committing it would put that in the git history
# permanently: readable by everyone with repository access, copied into every
# clone, and impossible to remove if one of them ever asks to be erased —
# which the nFADP entitles them to and docs/DATA-RETENTION.md is otherwise
# careful about.
#
# The importer reads this at run time and validates it, rather than
# importing it as a module: a TypeScript import of an ignored file would
# break `tsc --noEmit` on a clean clone.
#
# The database is where this data belongs. Keep the file locally, run
# `pnpm db:import-legacy --apply` once, then archive it wherever the signed
# PDFs themselves are kept.
/scripts/legacy-contracts.json

# Skills and agent definitions installed by the local tooling.
#
# Copied in by whichever assistant is running — they are that tool's cache,
# not this project's source, and they change with the tool rather than with
# the code. Committing them would make every clone carry one developer's
# choice of editor, and the lockfile beside them would conflict on every
# machine that refreshed at a different hour.
/.agents/
/.claude/
/skills-lock.json

# Screenshots taken by `pnpm shot`.
#
# Working images for looking at a change, not a record of one: they are
# regenerated whenever somebody wants to see a page, and a directory of stale
# PNGs of an interface that has since moved on is worse than none.
/screenshots/
